# TYKERF's AI settings: who answers in the editor's AI tab.
#
# 1. Copy this file to .env, next to where you start TYKERF, or in the page or site folder.
#    .env is never committed and never sent to the browser.
# 2. Keep ONE of the setups below (A, B, C or D) and remove the # in front of its lines.
# 3. Restart TYKERF. The AI tab shows, next to Send, which runner and model answer.
#
# Every setup gets the same tools, the same limit to the selected block, and the same history and
# undo. The difference is who thinks, and who pays: your Claude account, or the key you give here.
# More in docs/user-guide/en/ai.md ("Which AI answers").
#
# Write values without quotes and keep comments on their own lines: a # after a value would be
# read as part of the value.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A. Your own Claude Code (the default): nothing to set.
#    TYKERF runs the `claude` command, signed in with your Claude subscription or Claude Code's own
#    key. Install it from https://claude.com/claude-code, run `claude`, then /login.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
TYKERF_RUNNER=claude-code

# Optional: the model Claude Code uses (e.g. sonnet, opus); empty = Claude Code's own choice.
# TYKERF_MODEL=sonnet


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# B. The Anthropic API, with an API key from https://console.anthropic.com
#    Usage is billed to that key. Default model: claude-sonnet-5-5 (claude-opus-5-5 is more
#    capable and costs more; claude-haiku-5-5 is quicker and cheaper).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TYKERF_RUNNER=anthropic
# TYKERF_API_KEY=sk-ant-...
# TYKERF_MODEL=claude-sonnet-5-5


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# C. An Anthropic-compatible endpoint: a proxy, or another provider's compatible API.
#    Same runner as B, with the endpoint's address. Most of these want the key as a Bearer token
#    (TYKERF_AUTH_TOKEN); if yours wants it as an API key, use TYKERF_API_KEY instead.
#    TYKERF_MODEL must be a model name that endpoint offers.
#
#    Example, Z.ai (GLM models). Check the address and current model names at
#    https://docs.z.ai (they change with new GLM versions):
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TYKERF_RUNNER=anthropic
# TYKERF_AUTH_TOKEN=your-z.ai-key
# TYKERF_API_BASE_URL=https://api.z.ai/api/anthropic
# TYKERF_MODEL=glm-5.1


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# D. Google's Gemini API, with a key from https://aistudio.google.com/apikey
#    Default model: gemini-pro-latest (set a fixed version, or a Flash model for speed and cost).
#    GEMINI_BASE_URL is only for a proxy or a compatible endpoint.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TYKERF_RUNNER=gemini
# GEMINI_API_KEY=your-gemini-key
# TYKERF_MODEL=gemini-pro-latest
# GEMINI_BASE_URL=


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Good to know
# - TYKERF also accepts the usual names: ANTHROPIC_API_KEY, ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN,
#   ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL and GOOGLE_API_KEY.
# - Claude Code (setup A) reads the ANTHROPIC_* variables too. To send Claude Code itself
#   through a proxy, keep TYKERF_RUNNER=claude-code and set:
#     ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=https://api.z.ai/api/anthropic
#     ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN=your-z.ai-key
#   For B and C, use the TYKERF_* names, so how Claude Code signs in doesn't change.
# - With B, C and D, one model turn may take 3 minutes; then the run stops and says so. A slow model
#   or endpoint can have longer, in seconds: TYKERF_TURN_TIMEOUT=300
# - A wrong key or model name shows up as a message in the AI tab ("the API key was refused",
#   "the model … was not found").
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Serving a site to visitors (see DEPLOY.md): without these, TYKERF is a local editor that
# everyone who reaches it can use. Behind an HTTPS proxy, TYKERF_PUBLIC_URL is the https:// address.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TYKERF_ADMIN_PASSWORD=a-long-passphrase
# TYKERF_PUBLIC_URL=https://cities.example.org

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Publishing a site to GitHub (the site manager's Publish; see DEPLOY.md). A fine-grained token
# with Contents: read and write on the site's repository only. Keep this file out of that repository.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TYKERF_GITHUB_TOKEN=github_pat_...
# TYKERF_GITHUB_API=https://api.github.com      # only for GitHub Enterprise
